From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 02:26:42 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] util: fall back to a container file when POSIX shm is denied

A sandboxed Apple application may only create POSIX shared memory under a
name belonging to an entitled application group, and an iOS application has
no such entitlement: shm_open() returns EPERM for every name. Measured in an
iOS app on macOS in Designed for iPad mode, where shm_open() is denied but an
mmap(MAP_SHARED) over a file in the app temporary directory works and carries
4 MiB through unchanged.

Fall back to an unlinked mkstemp file under TMPDIR, which inside an app
container is that container's own tmp directory. Callers only need a shareable
mapping, which this provides, and the file never appears in the namespace.
Platforms that can create POSIX shared memory keep doing so.

VIRGL_ANON_FILE_UNLINKED=1 asks an Apple host that can create POSIX shared
memory for the same unlinked file. An iOS application's file pages are
outside its memory footprint and POSIX shared memory is inside it, so a macOS
run that reads the footprint of a guest, whose texture exports and Venus
shared-memory blobs are such segments, reads high by the size of the
segments: 25 MiB at a 1133x687 display and 58 MiB at 2266x1374 for the
Android UI drawn by Venus, and 9 and 36 MiB for a Vulkan application's
swapchain. A measurement run sets it and reads what an iOS run would. It is
not a default, because the pages of a file can be written back to the volume
of the temporary directory. Unset, empty or "0", the host behaves as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/mesa/util/anon_file.c | 32 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/mesa/util/anon_file.c b/src/mesa/util/anon_file.c
index 4a48ef2db4ef0c6cfa2ca905dc9ec0c35b158c9f..3f482315b0b2b742a269dc0725fddad907f843d9 100644
--- a/src/mesa/util/anon_file.c
+++ b/src/mesa/util/anon_file.c
@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@
 #include <errno.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
 #include <limits.h>
+#include <stdbool.h>
 #include <string.h>
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
@@ -136,6 +137,19 @@ os_create_unlinked_file(off_t size, const char *tag)
    return fd;
 }
 
+#if defined(__APPLE__)
+/*
+ * Whether VIRGL_ANON_FILE_UNLINKED asks for unlinked files in place of POSIX
+ * shared memory on an Apple host: set and neither empty nor "0".
+ */
+static bool
+os_use_unlinked_files(void)
+{
+   const char *value = getenv("VIRGL_ANON_FILE_UNLINKED");
+   return value && value[0] && strcmp(value, "0") != 0;
+}
+#endif
+
 /*
  * Create a new, unique, anonymous file of the given size, and
  * return the file descriptor for it. The file descriptor is set
@@ -170,6 +184,15 @@ os_create_anonymous_file(off_t size, const char *debug_name)
    fd = shm_open(SHM_ANON, O_CREAT | O_RDWR | O_CLOEXEC, 0600);
 #elif defined(__APPLE__)
    const char *tag = (debug_name && debug_name[0]) ? debug_name : "mesa";
+   /* VIRGL_ANON_FILE_UNLINKED asks for the file an iOS application gets,
+    * where POSIX shared memory is denied.  Its pages belong to a file, which
+    * the memory accounting of an iOS application leaves out, while POSIX
+    * shared memory is anonymous and counted.  A macOS run that sets it reads
+    * the memory of the same guest as an iOS run, which is what a
+    * measurement wants.  It is not a default: those pages can be written
+    * back to the volume of the temporary directory. */
+   if (os_use_unlinked_files())
+      return os_create_unlinked_file(size, "mesa-shared");
    const unsigned int nonce = arc4random();
    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < 32; i++) {
      char shm_name[64];
@@ -196,6 +219,15 @@ os_create_anonymous_file(off_t size, const char *debug_name)
      if (errno != EEXIST)
        break;
    }
+   if (fd < 0 && (errno == EPERM || errno == EACCES)) {
+     /* A sandboxed Apple application is not allowed to create POSIX shared
+      * memory unless its name belongs to an entitled application group, and
+      * an iOS application has no such entitlement by default. An unlinked
+      * file in the process temporary directory -- inside the app container,
+      * on the same volume -- maps MAP_SHARED just as well, which is all a
+      * caller of this function needs. */
+     return os_create_unlinked_file(size, "mesa-shared");
+   }
 #elif defined(__OpenBSD__)
    char template[] = "/tmp/mesa-XXXXXXXXXX";
    fd = shm_mkstemp(template);
